# The exit checkpoint (§13; the witness S1.1 deferred): m's own field x -- declared in its body, the
# method's state (book §12) -- is written 7, then m throws.  What the activation wrote is published before
# the throw leaves, so E, after catching, reads 7 at the place of declaration, m\x; unpublished, the place
# would still hold the declaration's 0.  (Before Q52 the row wrote the unit's x from m: a bare write to a
# name m does not declare is its hidden input's -- the author, 2026-09-28 -- and publishes nothing.)
# Entry 7.
fn: m () int
    throws: Oops
    int: x 0
    x: 7
    throw: Oops()
return: 0

int: seen 0
m()
catch: Oops ()
    seen: m\x
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: seen; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
